.TH DEMO 1 @DATE@ "demo @VERSION@" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
demo \- exercise every roffsub element
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
\fBdemo\fR [\fB\-r\fR] [\fB\-k\fR \fIlist\fR] [\fIfile\fR ...]
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B demo
reads each \fIfile\fR in turn, or the standard input when none is named, and writes the records it finds in the order chosen by the options.
A space written as a backslash space, as in a\ b\ c, never breaks.
Fonts may change mid word, as in \fBsrt\fR(1) and \fIfile\fR.txt.
.PP
A second paragraph follows the first after one blank line, and it is long enough to wrap at every width the reference outputs use, which is the point of it.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-r\fR
reverse the emitted order.
.TP
\fB\-k\fR \fIlist\fR
sort by the columns in \fIlist\fR, then the remaining columns in order.
.TP
.B \-\-respect\-blanks
treat each indented block as a unit; the tag is longer than the indent, so the body starts on the next line.
.TP 4
.BR \-x
a width of four sets the prevailing indent, which the next item keeps.
.TP
.BI \-o " file"
write to \fIfile\fR instead of the standard output.
.SS Lists
.IP \(bu
bullets use the bullet character, shown as o on an ASCII terminal.
.IP \(bu
a second bullet item.
.IP 1. 4
a numbered item with its own width.
.IP 2.
a second numbered item.
.SS Nesting
.PP
Text before a nested block.
.RS
Nested text sits one prevailing indent further in.
.RS 3
A deeper block, moved in by three cells.
.RE
Back at the first level.
.RE
Back at the section margin.
.br
After a line break, still the same paragraph.
.sp 2
After two blank lines.
.SH TABLE
.TS
tab(@);
lb lb
l  l.
OPTION@MEANING
_
\fB\-r\fR@reverse the order
\fB\-k\fR \fIKEY\fR@sort on \fIKEY\fR
.TE
.PP
A boxed table with a text block:
.TS
box tab(@);
lb lb rb
l  l  r.
NAME@DESCRIPTION@SIZE
_
alpha@T{
a long description that has to wrap when the terminal is narrow, kept inside its column
T}@12
beta@short@3456
.TE
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
demo \-k5,3 file
demo \-r < input > output
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR srt (1),
.BR roffsub (7)
